1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:03,618
Before there was a city here, there was only this.

2
00:00:04,393 --> 00:00:08,916
Forest, ice, and a river that froze solid for half of every year.

3
00:00:09,691 --> 00:00:12,793
Nothing about this place promised greatness.

4
00:00:13,568 --> 00:00:17,008
The nearest thing to a road was the river itself.

5
00:00:17,783 --> 00:00:22,036
And here is the other thing you need to know before we begin.

6
00:00:22,811 --> 00:00:23,814
Moscow burns.

7
00:00:24,589 --> 00:00:30,128
Again and again across eight centuries, this city has been burned to the ground.

8
00:00:30,903 --> 00:00:33,463
And every single time, it grew back.

9
00:00:34,238 --> 00:00:35,919
That is the real story.

10
00:00:36,694 --> 00:00:38,442
Not conquest, not glory.

11
00:00:39,217 --> 00:00:42,996
Survival, and the stubborn habit of rebuilding on ash.

12
00:00:43,770 --> 00:00:48,565
In the twelfth century, this was the edge of the known Russian world.

13
00:00:49,339 --> 00:00:52,577
Dense forest, frozen marsh, and trading paths.

14
00:00:53,352 --> 00:00:56,454
A borderland that mattered to almost nobody.

15
00:00:57,228 --> 00:01:02,226
Kyiv was the centre of Russian life then, and Kyiv was far to the south.

16
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:11,856
In eleven forty seven, a prince named Yuri Dolgoruky called a meeting at a small wooden outpost on a hill above the Moskva river.

17
00:01:12,631 --> 00:01:16,883
That single line is the first time Moscow appears in writing.

18
00:01:17,658 --> 00:01:20,083
He was inviting an ally to dinner.

19
00:01:20,858 --> 00:01:24,163
The chronicle records the meal, not a founding.

20
00:01:24,938 --> 00:01:26,889
He did not found a capital.

21
00:01:27,664 --> 00:01:29,412
He fortified a waypoint.

22
00:01:30,187 --> 00:01:35,455
A timber wall, a few hundred people, and a good position between two rivers.

23
00:01:36,230 --> 00:01:37,978
That was all Moscow was.

24
00:01:38,753 --> 00:01:41,111
Its value was traffic, not power.

25
00:01:41,885 --> 00:01:46,003
And a wooden town in open forest has one defining weakness.

26
00:01:46,778 --> 00:01:50,556
It cannot survive fire, and it cannot survive cavalry.

27
00:01:51,331 --> 00:01:54,433
Within a century it would face both at once.

28
00:01:55,208 --> 00:02:01,491
In twelve thirty seven, riders came out of the east carrying news nobody wanted to believe.

29
00:02:02,266 --> 00:02:07,737
Something enormous was moving across the steppe, and it was moving toward them.

30
00:02:08,512 --> 00:02:13,306
They had perhaps a year of warning, and it made no difference at all.

31
00:02:14,081 --> 00:02:16,980
The Mongols under Batu Khan did not raid.

32
00:02:17,755 --> 00:02:18,691
They erased.

33
00:02:19,466 --> 00:02:26,561
Russian city after Russian city fell in weeks, and the armies sent to stop them simply ceased to exist.

34
00:02:27,336 --> 00:02:30,709
Riazan refused to surrender and was annihilated.

35
00:02:31,484 --> 00:02:35,330
Vladimir, seat of the grand prince, fell in three days.

36
00:02:36,105 --> 00:02:40,019
Moscow's turn came in the winter of twelve thirty eight.

37
00:02:40,794 --> 00:02:42,272
The walls were wood.

38
00:02:43,046 --> 00:02:44,727
The defenders were few.

39
00:02:45,502 --> 00:02:49,010
The town was taken and burned in a matter of days.

40
00:02:49,785 --> 00:02:54,850
It was not considered important enough to be remembered as a great siege.

41
00:02:55,625 --> 00:02:58,388
What remained was ash on frozen ground.

42
00:02:59,163 --> 00:03:03,213
For any ordinary settlement that is the end of the record.

43
00:03:03,987 --> 00:03:07,631
A name in a chronicle, and nothing written after it.

44
00:03:08,406 --> 00:03:10,763
But people came back to the hill.

45
00:03:11,538 --> 00:03:17,957
They rebuilt in timber, knowing it would burn again, because the position was worth the risk.

46
00:03:18,731 --> 00:03:22,443
That decision made everything that followed possible.

47
00:03:23,217 --> 00:03:26,590
Timber was cheap, and the rivers still met here.

48
00:03:27,365 --> 00:03:32,092
For the next two hundred years, Russia lived under the Golden Horde.

49
00:03:32,866 --> 00:03:34,412
Princes did not rule.

50
00:03:35,186 --> 00:03:37,950
They paid, they knelt, and they waited.

51
00:03:38,725 --> 00:03:45,820
The Horde issued a document called the yarlyk, and whichever prince held it ruled on the Khan's behalf.

52
00:03:46,595 --> 00:03:50,848
And this is where Moscow does something genuinely unexpected.

53
00:03:51,623 --> 00:03:54,454
It did not rise by fighting the Mongols.

54
00:03:55,229 --> 00:03:57,248
It rose by working for them.

55
00:03:58,023 --> 00:04:02,140
Ivan the first earned a nickname that tells you everything.

56
00:04:02,915 --> 00:04:03,512
Kalita.

57
00:04:04,287 --> 00:04:05,020
Moneybag.

58
00:04:05,795 --> 00:04:12,552
He persuaded the Horde to let Moscow collect the tribute owed by every other Russian principality.

59
00:04:13,327 --> 00:04:16,496
It was a tax farm, and Moscow was running it.

60
00:04:17,271 --> 00:04:21,998
Every year the silver passed through Moscow's hands on its way east.

61
00:04:22,773 --> 00:04:26,958
And every year a portion of it did not continue the journey.

62
00:04:27,732 --> 00:04:30,428
The city grew rich on the arrangement.

63
00:04:31,203 --> 00:04:35,997
The city bought land, absorbed its neighbours, and paid for churches.

64
00:04:36,772 --> 00:04:41,634
Within two generations it was the strongest principality in the north.

65
00:04:42,409 --> 00:04:46,594
By thirteen eighty, Moscow was strong enough to stop paying.

66
00:04:47,369 --> 00:04:52,366
Prince Dmitry met the Horde's army at Kulikovo, on open ground, and won.

67
00:04:53,141 --> 00:04:58,950
The Horde had fractured into rival factions, and Dmitry judged the moment correctly.

68
00:04:59,725 --> 00:05:04,790
It was the first time a Russian army had beaten the Mongols in the field.

69
00:05:05,565 --> 00:05:08,125
A psychological wall broke that day.

70
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:11,258
But be careful with this victory.

71
00:05:12,032 --> 00:05:16,014
He took the name Donskoy from the river he fought beside.

72
00:05:16,789 --> 00:05:20,974
Two years later the Horde came back and burned Moscow again.

73
00:05:21,749 --> 00:05:28,100
Real independence took another full century, and when it came, it came under Ivan the third.

74
00:05:28,210 --> 00:05:34,267
In fourteen eighty, Ivan refused the tribute and faced the Horde across the Ugra river.

75
00:05:35,048 --> 00:05:36,468
Both armies waited.

76
00:05:37,249 --> 00:05:39,965
Then the Mongols turned and rode away.

77
00:05:40,745 --> 00:05:44,143
Two and a half centuries ended without a battle.

78
00:05:44,924 --> 00:05:52,550
It is remembered as the Great Stand on the Ugra, and it is one of the quietest endings to an empire on record.

79
00:05:53,330 --> 00:05:55,842
Ivan then did something deliberate.

80
00:05:56,622 --> 00:06:03,566
He demolished the old white stone Kremlin and brought Italian architects to rebuild it in red brick.

81
00:06:04,347 --> 00:06:07,199
Those are the walls you still see today.

82
00:06:07,979 --> 00:06:16,764
The Italians brought the brick, the engineering, and the swallowtail battlements you can still count along the top of the wall.

83
00:06:17,545 --> 00:06:24,080
In fifteen forty seven his grandson was crowned with a title no Russian ruler had used before.

84
00:06:24,860 --> 00:06:25,735
Not prince.

85
00:06:26,516 --> 00:06:26,981
Tsar.

86
00:06:27,762 --> 00:06:29,319
From the word Caesar.

87
00:06:30,099 --> 00:06:32,270
This was not a change of rank.

88
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:36,925
It was a claim to inherit the authority of Rome itself.

89
00:06:37,706 --> 00:06:40,558
Moscow now claimed to be the third Rome.

90
00:06:41,339 --> 00:06:49,306
The first fell to barbarians, the second to the Ottomans, and this city declared itself the last one left standing.

91
00:06:50,086 --> 00:06:53,620
The man who took that title was sixteen years old.

92
00:06:54,401 --> 00:06:56,094
He began as a reformer.

93
00:06:56,875 --> 00:07:01,636
He ended as something the Russian language gave a permanent name to.

94
00:07:02,417 --> 00:07:09,702
His mother was poisoned when he was eight, and he grew up surrounded by the men he suspected of doing it.

95
00:07:10,482 --> 00:07:11,698
Ivan the fourth.

96
00:07:12,479 --> 00:07:13,081
Grozny.

97
00:07:13,861 --> 00:07:20,260
Usually translated as terrible, though the word sits closer to formidable, or awe inspiring.

98
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:23,892
Both readings turned out to be accurate.

99
00:07:24,673 --> 00:07:25,752
He built this.

100
00:07:26,533 --> 00:07:36,000
Saint Basil's cathedral, raised to mark the conquest of Kazan, is the most recognisable building in Russia, and it came out of his reign.

101
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:41,133
Nine chapels on a single foundation, and no two of them alike.

102
00:07:41,914 --> 00:07:44,016
He also built the oprichnina.

103
00:07:44,796 --> 00:07:53,377
A private force answering to him alone, with authority to seize, torture and execute without explanation and without appeal.

104
00:07:54,157 --> 00:08:00,760
They rode in black, on black horses, carrying a broom and a severed dog's head as their emblem.

105
00:08:01,541 --> 00:08:05,553
For seven years the state made war on its own population.

106
00:08:06,333 --> 00:08:08,231
Entire cities were purged.

107
00:08:09,011 --> 00:08:14,387
Novgorod, a rival trading power, was broken so thoroughly it never recovered.

108
00:08:15,167 --> 00:08:23,066
And while the Tsar was hunting enemies inside his own borders, the real enemy was quietly assembling outside them.

109
00:08:23,846 --> 00:08:31,268
In fifteen seventy one the Crimean Tatars rode north, went around the army entirely, and set Moscow alight.

110
00:08:32,048 --> 00:08:35,582
The city burned almost completely in a single day.

111
00:08:36,363 --> 00:08:40,920
The death toll was enormous, and the estimates still vary widely.

112
00:08:41,701 --> 00:08:43,394
The Kremlin walls held.

113
00:08:44,174 --> 00:08:46,481
Almost nothing outside them did.

114
00:08:47,262 --> 00:08:49,705
Ivan's dynasty died with his sons.

115
00:08:50,486 --> 00:08:53,679
What followed is called the Time of Troubles.

116
00:08:54,459 --> 00:09:01,062
Famine, pretenders, civil war, and finally a Polish garrison sitting inside the Kremlin itself.

117
00:09:01,843 --> 00:09:06,605
By sixteen eleven Moscow was occupied, burned again, and leaderless.

118
00:09:07,385 --> 00:09:12,897
This is the closest Russia has ever come to simply ceasing to exist as a state.

119
00:09:13,678 --> 00:09:21,031
A Polish garrison held the fortress, a Swedish army held the north, and there was no agreed Tsar anywhere.

120
00:09:21,811 --> 00:09:24,800
The recovery did not come from the throne.

121
00:09:25,580 --> 00:09:35,320
It came from a merchant in Nizhny Novgorod, a butcher named Kuzma Minin, who raised the money and the men alongside a prince named Pozharsky.

122
00:09:36,101 --> 00:09:41,817
They marched on Moscow with an army raised by public subscription, not by a crown.

123
00:09:42,598 --> 00:09:45,586
In sixteen twelve they retook the Kremlin.

124
00:09:46,367 --> 00:09:53,584
A new dynasty, the Romanovs, took the throne the following year, and held it for the next three hundred.

125
00:09:54,364 --> 00:09:58,649
The day they entered the Kremlin is still a national holiday.

126
00:09:59,429 --> 00:10:04,191
And then Moscow lost the one thing it had spent centuries acquiring.

127
00:10:04,971 --> 00:10:10,892
In seventeen twelve, Peter the Great moved the capital to his new city on the Baltic.

128
00:10:11,673 --> 00:10:14,594
Saint Petersburg was built facing Europe.

129
00:10:15,374 --> 00:10:17,954
Moscow was left facing its own past.

130
00:10:18,734 --> 00:10:22,200
For two hundred years Moscow was the old capital.

131
00:10:22,981 --> 00:10:28,084
Respected, ceremonial, and no longer the place where decisions were made.

132
00:10:28,864 --> 00:10:30,353
The city went quiet.

133
00:10:31,133 --> 00:10:34,531
Which is what makes the next chapter so strange.

134
00:10:35,312 --> 00:10:42,119
The most famous event in Moscow's entire history happened while Moscow was not the capital at all.

135
00:10:42,900 --> 00:10:44,115
Eighteen twelve.

136
00:10:44,896 --> 00:10:50,953
The largest army Europe had ever assembled crossed into Russia with a single objective.

137
00:10:51,734 --> 00:10:55,200
Force one decisive battle, and dictate the peace.

138
00:10:55,310 --> 00:10:58,551
Napoleon brought more than half a million men.

139
00:10:59,327 --> 00:11:02,974
The Russian armies refused him the battle he needed.

140
00:11:03,750 --> 00:11:08,821
They retreated instead, and burned the supplies behind them as they went.

141
00:11:09,597 --> 00:11:14,532
It was the largest invasion force assembled in Europe up to that point.

142
00:11:15,308 --> 00:11:20,446
He reached Moscow in September and found something no conqueror plans for.

143
00:11:21,222 --> 00:11:22,633
The city was empty.

144
00:11:23,409 --> 00:11:26,108
Almost the entire population had left.

145
00:11:26,884 --> 00:11:29,921
There was nobody there to surrender to him.

146
00:11:30,697 --> 00:11:33,803
A conqueror needs somebody to accept defeat.

147
00:11:34,578 --> 00:11:37,277
There was nobody left in the building.

148
00:11:38,053 --> 00:11:41,904
That night, fires started in several districts at once.

149
00:11:42,680 --> 00:11:48,022
The city's firefighting equipment had been removed before the French arrived.

150
00:11:48,797 --> 00:11:51,971
Most of the city was still built out of wood.

151
00:11:52,746 --> 00:11:56,665
Over four days, roughly three quarters of Moscow burned.

152
00:11:57,441 --> 00:12:03,257
Whether an order was given, or the fires spread from looting, is argued to this day.

153
00:12:04,033 --> 00:12:06,122
The effect was not ambiguous.

154
00:12:06,897 --> 00:12:12,104
The governor of the city had ordered the fire pumps removed before he left.

155
00:12:12,879 --> 00:12:16,595
Napoleon held the capital and gained nothing from it.

156
00:12:17,370 --> 00:12:21,831
No supplies, no winter shelter, no government to negotiate with.

157
00:12:22,607 --> 00:12:26,661
He waited five weeks for a peace offer that never arrived.

158
00:12:27,437 --> 00:12:29,526
The retreat began in October.

159
00:12:30,302 --> 00:12:35,169
Of the men who marched into Russia, only a small fraction marched out.

160
00:12:35,945 --> 00:12:42,778
The army that had beaten every power in Europe was destroyed by a city that refused to be occupied.

161
00:12:43,553 --> 00:12:48,285
Cold, hunger and constant attack finished what the fire had started.

162
00:12:49,061 --> 00:12:52,776
A century later the empire collapsed from the inside.

163
00:12:53,552 --> 00:12:59,368
Nineteen seventeen brought revolution, and with it a decision that reversed Peter's.

164
00:13:00,144 --> 00:13:04,944
The Bolsheviks moved the capital back to Moscow in nineteen eighteen.

165
00:13:05,719 --> 00:13:09,503
The imperial eagles came down from the Kremlin towers.

166
00:13:10,278 --> 00:13:13,723
Red Square became the stage of a global ideology.

167
00:13:14,498 --> 00:13:20,789
Moscow was now the centre of a state that intended to remake the entire world in its image.

168
00:13:21,565 --> 00:13:26,974
Foreign delegations came to study it, and foreign governments came to fear it.

169
00:13:27,750 --> 00:13:34,379
In the nineteen thirties the eagles were replaced with red stars of ruby glass, lit from inside.

170
00:13:35,155 --> 00:13:39,141
They are still up there, and they still turn in the wind.

171
00:13:39,917 --> 00:13:47,631
And then, for the fourth time in its history, an army came for this city with the stated intention of ending it.

172
00:13:48,406 --> 00:13:54,358
In the autumn of nineteen forty one, German forces advanced to within sight of Moscow.

173
00:13:55,134 --> 00:13:59,459
Some units reached the last tram stop on the edge of the city.

174
00:14:00,235 --> 00:14:05,306
Officers could see the buildings of the city through their field glasses.

175
00:14:06,081 --> 00:14:08,374
The city did not fully evacuate.

176
00:14:09,149 --> 00:14:10,832
Factories kept running.

177
00:14:11,608 --> 00:14:18,779
Civilians dug anti tank ditches by hand, and in December, in extreme cold, the Red Army counterattacked.

178
00:14:19,555 --> 00:14:27,675
The military parade on Red Square went ahead that November, and the troops marched from it directly to the front line.

179
00:14:28,451 --> 00:14:31,150
The advance stopped there permanently.

180
00:14:31,925 --> 00:14:40,520
It was the first major failure of that army, and it happened at the gates of a city that had already survived three burnings.

181
00:14:41,296 --> 00:14:44,943
What followed was construction on an enormous scale.

182
00:14:45,719 --> 00:14:51,942
Wide avenues, a metro built like a palace, and seven towers that still define the skyline.

183
00:14:52,717 --> 00:14:58,534
Much of it was raised by a population that had just lost tens of millions of people.

184
00:14:59,309 --> 00:15:09,395
And in December nineteen ninety one the flag above the Kremlin came down, and the state that had built all of it ceased to exist, almost overnight.

185
00:15:10,170 --> 00:15:11,988
Moscow absorbed that too.

186
00:15:12,764 --> 00:15:23,392
Today it holds more than twelve million people, and the towers of its financial district stand a few kilometres from walls raised in the fourteen hundreds.

187
00:15:24,167 --> 00:15:29,170
The two sit inside the same skyline, and neither one looks out of place.

188
00:15:29,946 --> 00:15:34,610
Look at the whole sequence at once and the pattern is hard to miss.

189
00:15:35,386 --> 00:15:37,001
Burned by the Mongols.

190
00:15:37,776 --> 00:15:39,323
Burned by the Tatars.

191
00:15:40,099 --> 00:15:41,714
Occupied by the Poles.

192
00:15:42,489 --> 00:15:44,782
Burned again in eighteen twelve.

193
00:15:45,558 --> 00:15:47,782
Besieged in nineteen forty one.

194
00:15:48,558 --> 00:15:51,189
Five times destroyed or nearly taken.

195
00:15:51,965 --> 00:16:00,220
Five times rebuilt, on the same hill, above the same river, by people who kept deciding the position was worth the risk.

196
00:16:00,996 --> 00:16:03,831
That is not really a story about empire.

197
00:16:04,606 --> 00:16:12,794
It is a story about a decision made over and over, usually by ordinary people standing in ash, choosing to start again.

198
00:16:13,570 --> 00:16:15,591
Moscow was never inevitable.

199
00:16:16,367 --> 00:16:17,507
It was rebuilt.

200
00:16:18,283 --> 00:16:26,200
Thank you for watching, and if you want the next chapter of this history, tell us in the comments where to go next.

